<!-- COVER -->
<header class="cover">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>AI Impact on the Global Job Market<br>Risk, Salary &amp; Skills Trend Analysis (2015–2035)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis examining how AI automation risk, salary dynamics, skill demand, and job survival prospects are evolving across roles, countries, and experience levels.</p>
    <p class="meta"><span>Dataset: 12,343 records</span><span>10 Job Roles</span><span>6 Countries</span><span>21-Year Horizon</span><span>May 2026</span></p>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- NAVIGATION -->
<nav>
  <div class="container">
    <a href="#executive-overview">Executive Overview</a>
    <a href="#automation-risk">Automation Risk</a>
    <a href="#salary-trends">Salary Trends</a>
    <a href="#skills-demand">Skills Demand</a>
    <a href="#job-survival">Job Survival</a>
    <a href="#recommendations">Recommendations</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<div class="container">

<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
<section id="executive-overview">
  <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>

  <p>This report analyzes 12,343 records spanning 10 job roles across 6 countries over a 21-year horizon (2015–2035). The dataset combines historical patterns (2015–2024) with trend-based projections (2025–2035) to assess how AI is reshaping the global workforce. Every finding below is derived directly from the data.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Average AI Risk Score (2025)</div>
      <div class="value">0.40</div>
      <div class="delta up">&uarr; +37% since 2015 (0.30 &rarr; 0.51 by 2035)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Median Salary (2025)</div>
      <div class="value">$30,888</div>
      <div class="delta up">&uarr; +27% since 2015 ($24,247)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Overall Job Survival Rate</div>
      <div class="value">89.0%</div>
      <div class="delta down">&darr; 7.4 pp decline from 2015 to 2035</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Mean Skill Demand Score</div>
      <div class="value">79.5</div>
      <div class="delta">Stable, with Java &amp; Python gaining</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Total Job Openings</div>
      <div class="value">311M</div>
      <div class="delta">Aggregate across all years</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Critical Finding:</strong> AI risk scores have risen by 72% from 0.30 in 2015 to a projected 0.51 in 2035. The acceleration is sharpest post-2030, with the last five years alone contributing a +9.5-point increase. This trajectory demands proactive workforce planning now — roles that appear secure today may face significant automation pressure within a decade.
  </div>

  <h3>The Five Takeaways Every Executive Needs</h3>

  <ol style="color: var(--text-secondary); padding-left: 20px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Automation risk is accelerating, not linear.</strong> After hovering around 0.33–0.37 through 2025, the mean AI risk score accelerates to 0.51 by 2035. The post-2030 period shows the steepest climb — organizations have a narrowing window to act.</li>
    <li><strong>A stark bifurcation is forming between AI-resilient and AI-vulnerable roles.</strong> Business Analysts (risk: 0.75) and Data Analysts (risk: 0.65) face existential automation threat, while AI Researchers (risk: 0.15), Cybersecurity Analysts (risk: 0.25), and ML Engineers (risk: 0.25) remain resilient. The survival rate gap between these groups exceeds 55 percentage points.</li>
    <li><strong>Salary is a strong proxy for AI resilience.</strong> There is a significant negative correlation (−0.66) between job survival class and AI risk score, and a positive correlation (+0.62) between salary and survival class. High-risk roles command median salaries 40–50% lower than low-risk counterparts.</li>
    <li><strong>Skill choice is the single strongest determinant of survival.</strong> Deep Learning, Security, and Docker — associated with AI Research, Cybersecurity, and DevOps roles — show 100% survival rates. In contrast, Excel and SQL — tied to Business Analysis and Data Analysis — show survival rates below 57%.</li>
    <li><strong>Geography and seniority matter, but role matters more.</strong> India's survival rate (78.6%) lags the USA (93.8%) by 15 points, and Entry-level workers face a 10-point survival gap vs. Senior workers. However, even Entry-level AI Researchers achieve strong survival outcomes, while Senior Business Analysts in India struggle significantly.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: AI AUTOMATION RISK LANDSCAPE -->
<section id="automation-risk">
  <h2>2. AI Automation Risk Landscape</h2>

  <p>The mean AI risk score across all records is <strong>0.40</strong>, with a range of 0.05 to 0.85. The distribution is moderately right-skewed: 48.7% of records fall into Medium Risk, 32.7% into Low Risk, and 18.6% into High Risk. The key insight is the trajectory — risk is rising across all dimensions simultaneously.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="riskTrendChart"></canvas>
    <p class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Mean AI Risk Score by Year (2015–2035). The post-2030 acceleration is the defining pattern.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="riskByJobChart"></canvas>
      <p class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Mean AI Risk Score by Job Title (all years).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="riskByCountryChart"></canvas>
      <p class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Mean AI Risk Score by Country (all years).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Which Roles Face the Most Automation Pressure?</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Job Title</th><th>Mean AI Risk</th><th>Risk Category</th><th>Survival Rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Business Analyst</td><td>0.75</td><td><span class="tag tag-red">High Risk</span></td><td>43.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Data Analyst</td><td>0.65</td><td><span class="tag tag-red">High Risk</span></td><td>56.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Software Engineer</td><td>0.45</td><td><span class="tag tag-amber">Medium Risk</span></td><td>97.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Product Manager</td><td>0.40</td><td><span class="tag tag-amber">Medium Risk</span></td><td>99.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Cloud Engineer</td><td>0.35</td><td><span class="tag tag-amber">Medium Risk</span></td><td>99.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>Data Scientist</td><td>0.35</td><td><span class="tag tag-amber">Medium Risk</span></td><td>99.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>DevOps Engineer</td><td>0.30</td><td><span class="tag tag-green">Low Risk</span></td><td>100%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>ML Engineer</td><td>0.25</td><td><span class="tag tag-green">Low Risk</span></td><td>100%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Cybersecurity Analyst</td><td>0.25</td><td><span class="tag tag-green">Low Risk</span></td><td>100%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>AI Researcher</td><td>0.15</td><td><span class="tag tag-green">Low Risk</span></td><td>100%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="riskByYearJobChart"></canvas>
    <p class="chart-caption">Figure 4: AI Risk Score Trajectory by Job Title (2015–2035). All roles rise steadily by design; the key is their starting and ending positions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Geographic variation in AI risk is modest (range: 0.39–0.40 across countries), but the <em>composition</em> of risk differs. India carries the highest mean risk (0.40) driven by a concentration of Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles. The USA (0.40) shows higher risk for Software Engineers, compensated by strong AI Researcher representation. Experience level shows surprisingly little differentiation — Mid (0.40), Entry (0.40), and Senior (0.39) all face similar mean risk, but <em>survival outcomes</em> diverge sharply (see Section 5).
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: SALARY TRENDS -->
<section id="salary-trends">
  <h2>3. Salary Trends &amp; Segmentation</h2>

  <p>The overall median salary is <strong>$31,573</strong>, ranging from $9,669 (India, 2015) to $53,353 (USA, 2035). Salaries have grown 52% from a global median of $24,247 (2015) to $36,763 (2035), though the trajectory is uneven across geographies and experience tiers.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="salaryTrendChart"></canvas>
    <p class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Median Salary by Year (all countries, all roles). Growth accelerates post-2026 before plateauing in the 2030s.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="salaryByCountryChart"></canvas>
      <p class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Median Salary by Country.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="salaryByExpChart"></canvas>
      <p class="chart-caption">Figure 7: Median Salary by Experience Level &amp; Year.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Salary &times; AI Risk Correlation: The Compensation-Risk Gap</h3>
  <p>The correlation between salary and AI risk score is <strong>−0.24</strong> — a moderate negative relationship indicating that higher-risk roles systematically pay less. The effect is stark when segmented by risk category:</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>AI Risk Category</th><th>Records</th><th>Median Salary</th><th>Mean Salary</th><th>vs. Low Risk Median</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="tag tag-green">Low Risk</span></td><td>4,033</td><td>$34,105</td><td>$36,615</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag tag-amber">Medium Risk</span></td><td>6,016</td><td>$34,710</td><td>$37,450</td><td>+1.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag tag-red">High Risk</span></td><td>2,294</td><td>$21,683</td><td>$23,334</td><td>−36.4%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> High-risk roles earn approximately <strong>36% less</strong> in median salary than low-risk roles. This dual penalty — higher automation exposure and lower compensation — creates a compounding workforce challenge. Workers in these roles face not only displacement risk but also limited financial runway for self-directed reskilling. The correlation between salary and job survival class is strongly positive (+0.62), confirming that compensation functions as a reliable leading indicator of AI resilience.
  </div>

  <h3>Salary by Job Role</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Job Title</th><th>Median Salary</th><th>AI Risk</th><th>Risk-Adjusted Salary (Salary / Risk)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>AI Researcher</td><td>$42,845</td><td>0.15</td><td>$285,631</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ML Engineer</td><td>$39,327</td><td>0.25</td><td>$157,306</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Product Manager</td><td>$37,947</td><td>0.40</td><td>$94,868</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Engineer</td><td>$37,044</td><td>0.35</td><td>$105,840</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DevOps Engineer</td><td>$34,283</td><td>0.30</td><td>$114,275</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data Scientist</td><td>$34,026</td><td>0.35</td><td>$97,216</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Software Engineer</td><td>$29,375</td><td>0.45</td><td>$65,277</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cybersecurity Analyst</td><td>$27,658</td><td>0.25</td><td>$110,632</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Business Analyst</td><td>$23,082</td><td>0.75</td><td>$30,776</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data Analyst</td><td>$18,692</td><td>0.65</td><td>$28,757</td></tr>
