      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 1</div>
      <h4>Establish an AI-Resilience Reskilling Pipeline for Business Analysts and Data Analysts</h4>
      <p><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Business Analysts (AI risk: 0.75, rising to 0.85 by 2035) and Data Analysts (0.65 → 0.75) represent 21% of all records and face survival rates of just 43% and 57% respectively. These roles account for 66.7M total job openings — the largest combined volume — yet are on a clear trajectory toward AI displacement.<br><br>
      <strong>Action:</strong> Create structured transition pathways from these roles into adjacent, AI-resilient positions: Business Analyst → Product Manager (risk 0.40, survival 99.5%); Data Analyst → Data Scientist (risk 0.35, survival 99.8%). The skill delta is manageable: Python proficiency and strategic thinking for the former, machine learning fundamentals for the latter.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 2</div>
      <h4>Weight Talent Investment Toward Skills, Not Titles</h4>
      <p><strong>Data Basis:</strong> Skill demand scores are nearly uniform across all nine primary skills (range: 78.9–79.9), but survival rates range from 43% (Excel) to 100% (Deep Learning). Demand scores are essentially non-informative as standalone signals — the correlation between skill demand and job survival is only +0.27, far weaker than the role-survival link (−0.66 for AI risk).<br><br>
      <strong>Action:</strong> Build hiring and L&amp;D frameworks around <em>skill-role pairs</em> rather than skills in isolation. Python + Data Science = resilience; Python + Data Analysis = vulnerability. Investment in AI/ML, security, and DevOps competencies delivers the highest survival ROI regardless of initial role.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 3</div>
      <h4>Concentrate Reskilling Investment in India and Other High-Risk Geographies</h4>
      <p><strong>Data Basis:</strong> India exhibits a 78.6% survival rate — 15 points below the USA — driven by a heavy concentration of Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles and the lowest median salary ($12,448) among all countries. Entry-level Business Analysts in India have a near-zero survival class (0.03) with a median salary of $6,436.<br><br>
      <strong>Action:</strong> Prioritize reskilling programs in India, targeting the Business Analyst → Product Manager and Data Analyst → Data Scientist pathways. The wage uplift from successful transitions (>2&times; salary improvement) generates a strong ROI for training investment.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 4</div>
      <h4>Use Salary Premiums as an AI-Resilience Early Warning System</h4>
      <p><strong>Data Basis:</strong> The salary–survival correlation (+0.62) and the salary–AI risk correlation (−0.24) establish compensation as a reliable leading indicator of automation exposure. High-risk roles earn a 36% median salary penalty compared to low-risk roles. The correlation is stronger than skill demand or job opening volume.<br><br>
      <strong>Action:</strong> Integrate salary benchmarking into workforce risk assessments. Roles where median compensation is stagnant or declining relative to inflation — after controlling for experience and location — should be flagged for proactive reskilling review, even if current demand scores appear healthy.</p>
    </div>

